    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen peptides have reasonable evidence for reducing activity-related joint
        pain — better evidence, in fact, than glucosamine has as a standalone.</strong> The
        strongest single trial followed 147 athletes for 24 weeks on 10g of collagen hydrolysate
        daily and found significant reductions in joint pain when walking, standing and carrying
        objects. What to know before you buy:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Dose is the whole game.</strong> The positive trials used 5g–10g of collagen peptides daily. Most joint supplements contain a fraction of that.</li>
        <li><strong>Two different mechanisms exist.</strong> Hydrolysed collagen at 5–10g works by stimulating cartilage matrix production. Undenatured type II collagen at 40mg works through an entirely different immune pathway. They are not interchangeable, and neither is "better".</li>
        <li><strong>Timeline is 12–24 weeks.</strong> Cartilage turns over slowly. Every trial that showed benefit ran for at least three months.</li>
        <li><strong>It is not a painkiller.</strong> Collagen supports the tissue. It does not block pain signals, and it will not resolve a mechanical problem.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

