      <h2 id="why-joints-fail">Why joints deteriorate, and why repair is slow</h2>

      <p>
        Articular cartilage — the glassy white surface capping the ends of your bones — is one of
        the few tissues in the human body with <strong>no blood supply of its own</strong>. It has
        no arteries, no veins, no direct nerve supply. Everything it needs arrives by diffusion
        through synovial fluid, driven by the compression and release of the joint as you move.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two consequences follow, and both are clinically important. First, cartilage repairs
        extraordinarily slowly, which is why a cartilage injury behaves nothing like a muscle
        strain. Second, <strong>movement is not optional</strong> — a joint that is not loaded and
        unloaded regularly is a joint that is not being fed. Rest is often exactly the wrong
        prescription.
      </p>

      <p>
        Structurally, cartilage is roughly 70% water held in a matrix of type II collagen fibres
        and proteoglycans. The collagen network provides tensile strength; the proteoglycans draw
        in water and provide compressive cushioning. Osteoarthritis is, at the tissue level, the
        progressive fraying of that collagen network and the loss of the water it holds. Joint
        space narrows, the surfaces stop gliding, and the underlying bone — which does have nerves —
        starts registering load it was never meant to carry.
      </p>

