      <h2 id="type-i-vs-ii">Type I vs Type II collagen for joints</h2>

      <p>
        This is where most confusion — and most misleading marketing — lives. Type II is the
        collagen found in cartilage, so the intuitive conclusion is that a joint supplement must
        contain type II. That intuition is only half right, because <strong>there are two entirely
        separate mechanisms at work</strong>, and they operate at doses that differ by a factor of
        two hundred.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">Hydrolysed collagen peptides (Type I)</th>
              <th scope="col">Undenatured Type II (UC-II)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Typical dose</th><td>5,000–10,000mg daily</td><td>40mg daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Mechanism</th><td>Absorbed peptides signal chondrocytes to increase cartilage matrix production</td><td>Oral tolerance — trains the immune system to stop attacking joint cartilage</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Must it be intact?</th><td>No — it is deliberately broken down</td><td>Yes — heat or hydrolysis destroys the effect entirely</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Best evidence in</th><td>Activity-related joint pain, athletes, early-stage discomfort</td><td>Diagnosed knee osteoarthritis</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Also supports</th><td>Bone, tendon, ligament, skin</td><td>Cartilage only</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The practical takeaway: <strong>a product containing "type II collagen" at a gram-level dose
        is almost certainly hydrolysed</strong>, which means the type II designation is doing no
        work — hydrolysed type II behaves like any other collagen peptide. And a supplement
        promising both mechanisms in one capsule is describing something chemically difficult, since
        UC-II must remain undenatured to function.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/products/skelecoll">Skelecoll</a> takes the peptide route: <strong>10,000mg of
        Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen</strong> per 30ml shot — 10g of protein, matching the
        dose used in the Clark athlete trial rather than a token inclusion.
      </p>

      <h2 id="evidence">What the trials found</h2>

      <h3>Clark et al., 2008 — 147 athletes, 24 weeks</h3>
      <p>
        The most cited joint trial. Athletes at Penn State with activity-related joint pain took
        10g of collagen hydrolysate or placebo daily for 24 weeks. The collagen group reported
        <strong>significantly less joint pain at rest, when walking, when standing, when carrying
        objects and when lifting</strong>. It is not a small study, it ran long enough for cartilage
        to plausibly respond, and its endpoints were functional rather than laboratory measures.
      </p>

      <h3>Zdzieblik et al., 2017 — knee pain in young adults</h3>
      <p>
        Young adults with functional knee pain but no diagnosed arthritis took 5g of specific
        collagen peptides daily for 12 weeks. The treatment group showed
        <strong>significantly reduced activity-related knee pain</strong>. This trial matters
        because it addresses the largest group of people buying joint supplements — those with
        early discomfort rather than established disease.
      </p>

      <h3>Benito-Ruiz et al., 2009 — 24 weeks, joint discomfort</h3>
      <p>
        A multicentre trial of 10g daily collagen hydrolysate over 24 weeks reported improvement in
        joint comfort, with the largest effects in participants who had the most severe symptoms at
        baseline — a pattern that recurs across this literature.
      </p>

      <p>
        The honest summary: the effect is <strong>consistent, moderate and slow</strong>. Collagen
        peptides reliably beat placebo on pain and function scores in trials lasting three to six
        months. They do not regrow cartilage, no trial has demonstrated that, and any brand claiming
        it is inventing a result nobody has produced.
      </p>

