      <h2 id="vs-glucosamine">Collagen vs glucosamine: an honest comparison</h2>

      <p>
        Glucosamine has been the default joint supplement for thirty years, so it deserves a
        straight answer rather than a convenient one.
      </p>

      <p>
        The largest independent test was the <strong>GAIT trial</strong>, published in the
        <em>New England Journal of Medicine</em> in 2006. Nearly 1,600 patients with knee
        osteoarthritis took glucosamine, chondroitin, both, celecoxib or placebo for 24 weeks.
        The headline result: <strong>glucosamine and chondroitin were no better than placebo</strong>
        for the group overall. A pre-specified subgroup with moderate-to-severe pain did show
        benefit from the combination, but that finding was exploratory and has not been reliably
        reproduced.
      </p>

      <p>
        Since then, the evidence has stayed mixed. Some meta-analyses find a small benefit for
        pharmaceutical-grade glucosamine sulphate; others find effects indistinguishable from
        placebo. Several major clinical guidelines now decline to recommend it.
      </p>

      <p>So where does that leave a formula like Skelecoll, which contains glucosamine?</p>

      <p>
        Being precise about this matters. The glucosamine monotherapy trials used
        <strong>1,500mg daily</strong>. Skelecoll contains <strong>300mg of glucosamine HCl</strong>
        — this is a supporting co-factor within a stack built around a 10g collagen dose, not a
        standalone glucosamine therapy, and it should not be read as one. If you specifically want
        to trial high-dose glucosamine sulphate on its own merits, buy it on its own at 1,500mg and
        judge it over three months.
      </p>

      <p>
        The stack logic is different. Collagen peptides supply the signal and the raw material for
        matrix production; glucosamine and <strong>120mg of hyaluronic acid</strong> support the
        proteoglycan and lubrication side; calcium, D3 and magnesium address the bone underneath.
        The bet is on covering the whole joint structure adequately rather than one component
        maximally.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bone-side">The bone side of the equation</h2>

      <p>
        Joint pain is rarely only about cartilage. The bone beneath a worn joint remodels under
        abnormal load, and in India specifically there is a widespread deficiency that makes this
        worse.
      </p>

      <p>
        Studies of urban Indian populations have repeatedly reported <strong>vitamin D deficiency
        in 70–90% of adults</strong> — a striking figure for a country with abundant sunlight,
        driven by indoor work, sun avoidance, air pollution and skin pigmentation. Vitamin D
        controls how much dietary calcium is actually absorbed. Without it, calcium intake is
        largely academic.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen matters here too. Bone is roughly one-third organic matrix by weight, and
        <strong>about 90% of that matrix is type I collagen</strong> — the mineral gives bone its
        hardness, but the collagen gives it the flexibility not to shatter. König and colleagues
        found in 2018 that 5g of collagen peptides daily for 12 months
        <strong>increased bone mineral density</strong> in postmenopausal women with reduced BMD.
      </p>

      <p>
        Skelecoll pairs its collagen with <strong>250mg calcium, 600 IU vitamin D3 and 50mg
        magnesium</strong> for exactly this reason. Magnesium is the quiet one in that trio: it is
        required for the conversion of vitamin D into its active form, and it helps the muscles
        around a painful joint release rather than guard.
      </p>

