      <h2 id="timeline">A realistic timeline</h2>

      <p>
        Cartilage does not respond on a weekly basis. Set expectations accordingly, and judge the
        result at twelve weeks rather than two.
      </p>

      <figure class="jr-figure jr-figure--slide">
        <img src="{{ 'skelecoll-slide-5.webp' | asset_url }}"
             alt="Skelecoll timeline: weeks 1 to 2 morning stiffness eases, weeks 3 to 4 knees and shoulders feel looser, weeks 6 to 8 range of motion improves, week 12 and beyond long-term joint and bone support"
             width="800" height="1000" loading="lazy">
        <figcaption>
          The pattern most people report. Stiffness responds first, range of motion follows, and
          the structural benefit accrues over months of continuous use.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1–4 — stiffness.</strong> Morning stiffness and the first few steps out of a chair usually ease before pain intensity changes. This is often hydration and lubrication rather than cartilage.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 4–8 — function.</strong> Stairs, squatting, getting up from the floor. Function tends to improve ahead of resting pain.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 8–12 — pain scores.</strong> The window in which trials recorded statistically significant reductions. If nothing has changed by week 12, it is reasonable to conclude it is not working for you.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond 12 weeks — structural support.</strong> Bone and cartilage benefits accrue on a scale of months to a year. This is the phase the König bone data speaks to.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        One thing will influence your outcome more than any supplement: <strong>loading the joint
        appropriately</strong>. Cartilage is fed by movement. Quadriceps strength is one of the
        best-established protective factors for knee osteoarthritis. A collagen shot alongside a
        strength programme is a reasonable plan; a collagen shot instead of one is not.
      </p>

      <h2 id="when-to-see-surgeon">When a supplement is the wrong answer</h2>

      <p>
        I would rather lose a sale than have someone delay a diagnosis. See a doctor rather than
        buying a supplement if you have:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>A joint that is hot, red and swollen</strong> — this suggests infection or acute inflammatory arthritis and needs urgent assessment</li>
        <li><strong>Locking or giving way</strong> — usually a mechanical problem such as a meniscal tear, which no supplement addresses</li>
        <li><strong>Pain that wakes you at night</strong> or pain at complete rest</li>
        <li><strong>Sudden severe pain after an injury</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Multiple swollen joints with morning stiffness lasting over an hour</strong> — a pattern suggestive of inflammatory arthritis, which requires disease-modifying treatment</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Collagen is a reasonable intervention for age-related wear, activity-related discomfort and
        long-term structural support. It is not a treatment for any of the above.
      </p>

