        If you have persistent joint pain, a diagnosed joint condition, a fish or shellfish allergy,
        or take prescription medication, speak to a qualified healthcare professional before
        starting supplementation.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take for joint pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Trials showing reduced joint pain used 5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily
            for at least 12 weeks. Skelecoll provides 10,000mg (10g) per 30ml shot, matching the
            dose used in the 24-week athlete trial by Clark and colleagues.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is type II collagen better than type I for joints?</summary>
          <p>
            Neither is universally better — they work differently. Hydrolysed collagen peptides at
            5–10g daily stimulate cartilage matrix production, while undenatured type II collagen
            at around 40mg daily works through an immune tolerance pathway. A gram-level "type II"
            product is almost certainly hydrolysed, in which case the type designation is not
            meaningful.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen better than glucosamine for knee pain?</summary>
          <p>
            Collagen peptides currently have more consistent trial evidence. The large independent
            GAIT trial found glucosamine and chondroitin no better than placebo overall for knee
            osteoarthritis, while collagen peptide trials have repeatedly beaten placebo on pain
            and function over 12 to 24 weeks.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does collagen take to work for joints?</summary>
          <p>
            Expect 12 weeks before judging it. Stiffness often eases in the first month, function
            improves through weeks 4 to 8, and measurable pain reduction appeared at 8 to 12 weeks
            in published trials. Cartilage turnover is slow and there is no fast version of this.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen rebuild or regrow cartilage?</summary>
          <p>
            No trial has demonstrated cartilage regrowth from oral collagen. What the evidence
            supports is reduced pain and improved function, likely through increased cartilage
            matrix production and better joint lubrication. Any claim of regrowth goes beyond the
            published research.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I take collagen for joints and skin at the same time?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Both use hydrolysed collagen peptides, so they are complementary rather than
            conflicting. Skelecoll is dosed and co-formulated for joints and bone, Collabloom for
            skin. If your primary concern is joints, start with Skelecoll — it also supports
            skin, since type I collagen is common to both.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

